from __future__ import annotations

from thinking_core import render_long, render_note


def stop_ai_use_case() -> str:
    body = '''<p>Organizations are usually better at starting AI initiatives than stopping them.</p><p>Once a use case has a sponsor, a team, a prototype and some visible momentum, continuation starts to feel like the natural outcome.</p><p>That is where portfolio discipline becomes difficult.</p><p>The question is no longer whether the idea was reasonable when it started.</p><p>It is:</p><blockquote>Does the evidence still justify the next unit of investment?</blockquote><p>If the answer changes, the decision should be allowed to change with it.</p><h2>Experimentation should create evidence, not permanent projects</h2><p>Early AI work should contain uncertainty.</p><p>A discovery or pilot phase exists precisely because the organization does not yet know whether the problem is important enough, whether AI materially improves it, whether the data and operating conditions are realistic or whether people will change how they work.</p><p>So I do not expect every experiment to succeed.</p><p>I expect every experiment to teach us something useful enough to make the next decision better.</p><p>That includes evidence that says:</p><blockquote>Do not continue.</blockquote><p>A model can perform well and still support a weak business case.</p><p>A prototype can impress stakeholders and still demand more workflow change than the value justifies.</p><p>A use case can remain useful and still become a worse investment than another opportunity competing for the same people, data or funding.</p><p>Technical success is not an automatic right to continue.</p><h2>The trade-off is learning versus sunk cost</h2><p>Stopping too early is also a mistake.</p><p>AI initiatives often need iteration before the real problem becomes visible. Data limitations emerge. Users behave differently than expected. The workflow turns out to matter more than the model.</p><p>The question I use is whether the next step is still reducing a material uncertainty.</p><p>If another experiment could meaningfully change the investment decision, continue learning.</p><p>If the team is simply refining the solution without changing what leadership believes about value, adoption, risk or operability, continuation becomes harder to justify.</p><p>That is the difference between useful iteration and sunk-cost protection.</p><h2>Six signals that make me reopen the decision</h2><p>I would seriously reconsider continuation when one or more of these conditions appear.</p><p><strong>1. The problem is no longer important enough.</strong><br>Business priorities move. A use case does not deserve capacity forever simply because it once aligned with the strategy.</p><p><strong>2. The value mechanism is still unclear.</strong><br>If the team cannot explain which decision, workflow or behaviour changes and how that creates value, additional model refinement may be solving the wrong problem.</p><p><strong>3. Production conditions are unrealistic.</strong><br>A pilot may depend on manual preparation, exceptional data access, specialist intervention or user attention that cannot be sustained in normal operations.</p><p><strong>4. Adoption costs more than the outcome justifies.</strong><br>Some solutions are technically credible but require so much behavioural or process change that the expected benefit becomes unattractive.</p><p><strong>5. Risk and control requirements change the economics.</strong><br>Monitoring, assurance, human oversight or regulatory requirements may make a technically viable use case commercially weak.</p><p><strong>6. A stronger opportunity now exists.</strong><br>Portfolio decisions are relative. An initiative does not need to become bad before something else becomes a better use of the same capacity.</p><p>That last point matters.</p><p>Sometimes stopping is not a judgment that the work has failed.</p><p>It is simply a judgment that scarce resources now have a better destination.</p><h2>Continuation should be a decision, not the default</h2><p>At every meaningful investment point, I want four questions answered:</p><p>What uncertainty were we trying to reduce?</p><p>What did we learn?</p><p>What changed in our confidence?</p><p>What evidence would justify the next commitment?</p><p>From there, three outcomes should be legitimate:</p><p><strong>Continue. Change direction. Stop.</strong></p><p>If only continuation is culturally acceptable, experimentation eventually becomes accumulation.</p><h2>A stopped initiative should still create value</h2><p>Stopping should not mean forgetting.</p><p>The portfolio should capture which assumption failed, what was learned about the data, users, workflow, economics or technology, and whether anything can be reused elsewhere.</p><p>That institutional learning is part of the return on experimentation.</p><p>The organization should not pay twice to rediscover the same constraint six months later under a different use-case name.</p><div class="rule"><strong>Every AI initiative should know what evidence would cause us to stop before we become emotionally invested in continuing.</strong><p>Good portfolio leadership is not measured by how many AI initiatives survive. It is measured by whether scarce attention keeps moving toward the work with the strongest evidence and the clearest path to value.</p></div>'''
    aside = '''<span>Stop / continue check</span><strong>Six signals that should reopen the investment decision.</strong><div class="article-questions"><div>The problem is no longer important enough</div><div>The value mechanism is still unclear</div><div>Production conditions are unrealistic</div><div>Adoption costs more than the outcome justifies</div><div>Risk requirements change the economics</div><div>A stronger opportunity now exists</div></div><p>Continuation should be an evidence-based decision, not the default.</p>'''
    return render_long("stop_ai_use_case", body, aside)


def roi_diagnosed_too_late() -> str:
    return render_note(
        "roi_diagnosed_too_late",
        "ROI starts with the value mechanism, not the post-launch dashboard.",
        "I do not want the first serious ROI conversation to happen after an AI solution is live. By then, the team may know model performance, usage and delivery cost but still be unable to explain what business behavior was supposed to change. I prefer to define the chain early: baseline → changed decision or workflow → observable adoption → operational effect → business outcome. The numbers will mature as evidence develops, but the causal logic should exist before major investment. If we cannot describe how the use case is expected to create value at discovery stage, measuring more things after launch will not repair the missing business case.",
    )
